Iron Deficiency Anaemia
Young Adult & Adolecence
Progressive fatigue, Conjunctival pallor, Pica/Koilonychia, Microcytic hypochromic anaemia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-025
Reason for Visit: Acute syncopal episode followed by worsening exertional dyspnoea and severe palpitations upon minimal exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Housemate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult who experienced a fainting episode while climbing stairs, now presenting with a racing heart, extreme generalized weakness, and shortness of breath at rest. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to immediately evaluate hemodynamic stability, exclude an acute cardiac arrhythmia or occult active gastrointestinal haemorrhage, and determine if an emergency blood transfusion was required prior to routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I passed out on the stairs, my heart is pounding out of my chest, and I feel so weak I can't even lift my arms."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 6-month history of progressive generalized fatigue and physical weakness, which has dramatically worsened over the last two weeks. The patient reports severe exertional dyspnoea and decreased exercise tolerance. The acute presentation was triggered by a syncopal episode while ascending stairs, followed by resting tachycardia. Upon targeted questioning, the patient admits to a bizarre, unnatural craving for chewing ice (pica) and notes severe restless legs syndrome at night. The patient reports heavy, prolonged menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) for the past year but denies dark or bloody stools.
Functional Status: Bedbound post-syncope; severely limited by breathlessness upon attempting to sit up.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent traumatic syncope; profound functional decline impacting employment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears profoundly pale, lethargic, and is breathing rapidly while at rest.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals stark, noticeable pallor of the skin, conjunctivae, and palmar creases. Oral examination demonstrates angular stomatitis (cracking at the corners of the mouth) and an atrophic glossitis (a smooth, red tongue). Extremities exhibit distinct koilonychia (spooning of the fingernails). Cardiovascular auscultation reveals a bounding sinus tachycardia and a prominent, innocent systolic flow murmur loudest at the left sternal border. The respiratory exam is clear. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with no palpable hepatosplenomegaly. The physical presentation is classically aligned with the diagnostic tetrad of progressive fatigue, conjunctival pallor, pica/koilonychia, and highly suggests a profound microcytic hypochromic anaemia requiring urgent laboratory confirmation.
Environmental Observations: Cups of crushed ice are present on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Patient has not taken any over-the-counter iron supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Symptomatic Iron Deficiency Anaemia (likely secondary to menorrhagia) - Status: Acute decompensation
Differentials:

Acute Gastrointestinal Haemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary acute driver due to the chronic 6-month progressive history, the presence of long-term tissue changes (koilonychia), and the absence of melaena or haematemesis.

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of resting tachycardia, flow murmur, and specific mucocutaneous signs of iron depletion (pica, glossitis), rather than bradycardia and weight gain.

Primary Cardiac Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely as the tachycardia is sinus in nature, appropriate for the degree of clinical anaemia, and is accompanied by classic stigmata of profound iron deficiency.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Fall risk secondary to anoxic syncope)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute decompensation of a chronic, severe iron deficiency anaemia, culminating in anoxic syncope, resting tachycardia, and a high-output cardiac state (flow murmur). The profound conjunctival and palmar pallor, coupled with the inability to tolerate minor exertion without dyspnoea, suggests a critically low haemoglobin level. Delaying assessment until a routine appointment posed an unacceptable risk of a fatal hypoxic cardiac event or recurrent traumatic falls. The urgent home visit safely ruled out an acute primary cardiac event, identified the probable chronic etiology (menorrhagia), and facilitated immediate hemodynamic stabilization strategies and urgent pathology testing to determine if an emergency inpatient blood transfusion is required.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Ordered an urgent, priority FBC, Iron Studies (ferritin, TIBC), and blood group/hold to be drawn first thing in the morning by a mobile pathology service, to confirm the expected microcytic hypochromic anaemia. Advised strict bed rest until blood results are reviewed. Prescribed oral Ferrous Sulfate 325mg daily to commence immediately after blood is drawn.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the absorption of the oral iron supplement, take the tablet with a full glass of orange juice (Vitamin C) on an empty stomach, chemically creating an acidic environment that forces the iron across the gut wall. To prevent further syncopal falls, sit on the edge of the bed for a full 60 seconds before attempting to stand, physically allowing your compromised cardiovascular system time to pump enough oxygen-poor blood to your brain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mobile pathology collection booked for 0800 hours.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review via telehealth tomorrow afternoon to review the FBC; if haemoglobin is critically low (e.g., <70 g/L), immediate ED referral for an iron infusion or packed red blood cell transfusion will be mandated.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000) is required if the patient develops central chest pain, feels physically unable to catch their breath while lying completely still, or loses consciousness again.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical signs heavily indicate a profound lack of iron and red blood cells; exact blood counts dictate whether you need an infusion in the hospital or just oral tablets.
Clinical Course: If your blood counts are stable enough for oral tablets, it will take 3 to 4 weeks to notice a significant improvement in your energy and breathlessness.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you develop a heavy, crushing pain in the center of your chest, or if you feel like you are suffocating even when lying completely flat and still in bed.

